hundreds of thousands of uh teenagers  receiving their gcse results this  morning your message to those  who don't uh receive the increasingly  attainable higher grades  well my message to those young people is  there are lots of options available the  there's a helpline the national career  service helpline for them to that and to  to ring to find out get advice for what  to do next but there are a lot of  options  for those that don't uh get the grades  to go on to do a levels or t levels are  there all kinds of different vocational  qualifications or apprenticeships uh  that young people can consider they can  retake those gcses of course if they  wish to as well there's an autumn series  they can retake those exams in the  autumn uh if they wish to uh to take  them to take them and of course there's  an appeal process for young people who  feel that they've not been given the  right grade but today we should be  congratulating those hundreds of  thousands  of young people who have achieved so  much in very trying and difficult  circumstances over the last 18 months  and we should be paying tribute to all  those young people for what they've  achieved it's the culmination of not  just two years of the gcse course but 11  years of school education so  congratulations to everyone  who's receiving their grades today to  enable them to go on to the next phase  of their education whether that's t  levels or a levels or an apprenticeship  and also thank you to the teachers  who've done a magnificent job in  administering the teacher assessed grade  system with all the advice and guidance  that they've had from the exam boards  and the and the evidence that they've  not been uh that they have compiled to  determine the grades that have been  awarded today okay uh well given what we  saw with the a levels it looks as though  the kids will do very well 45  of a level results were either a or a  star this is gonna have to be um looked  at for next year isn't it because you  can't just have you know half of kids  getting a and a stars how do you then  differentiate between them going forward  is that something that's going to have  to be considered for next year  yes this this is an exceptional year uh  designed to to to make sure that despite  the pandemic and despite the fact that  we had to cancel exams because it  wouldn't have been fair for children  young people to sit exams and they've  had such different experiences of kovi  the different levels of self-isolation  and so on so the teacher assessed great  system is the best alternative to making  sure they can go on to the next phase of  their of their education or their  questions  but we have already with respect with  respect minister that's not my question  my question is not this year next year  you're going to have to refresh the  grading system for next year aren't you  yeah so we've already announced the the  the the adjustments to the exams we  expect all young people to be taking  exams uh in 2022 but we'll be making  adjustments to those exams to reflect  the fact that this cohort will have had  disruption to their education as well  and the regulator off quo will be saying  more about the grading standard that  will be used uh for those exams uh in  the autumn term  okay so just to clarify you will be  refreshing the grading system for next  year  no what i said was we'll be talking  about what the grading standard will be  for the 2022 exams we are looking also  in the longer term  about how to make sure that we get back  to the system we had before the pandemic  broke where we had year on year no great  inflation and we want to get back to  that system uh  in the longer run but we have to make  sure when we do that that we are fair  between different year different cohorts  of the ones that took their exams in  2020 and 2021 and the ones the students  are taking the exams in the future is  fairness that lies at the heart of any  decision uh any decisions about grading  these are very difficult decisions to  make and we will be doing so in  conjunction uh with the regulator with  the exam boards and indeed with teachers  and parents and students up and down the  country before we come to firm decisions  but but the  issue about the granting system is the  longer term we only have a limited  amount of time minister as you know and  i've allowed you very full some answers  to the first two questions you're  talking about being fair uh private  school uh pupils grades far higher than  those uh from less uh privileged  backgrounds you've been schools minister  since uh 2010  is it demoralizing for you with all the  work that you've done to close that  attainment gap to then see what's  happening  well we have closed retainment gap since  2010 by 13  in primary schools and by nine percent  and second up to the beginning of the of  the pandemic independent schools are  academically selective by and large uh  and they always do get the higher fair  higher share of top grades and this year  what we've seen is that right across the  school system whatever kind of school  the young person goes to they have seen  an increase in those top grades and if  you look at the relative change in those  top grades independent schools have gone  up by about 15 percent  comprehensive have gone up by 18 so  those top students are performing better  right across the board wherever whatever  type of school they go to but we are  determined as a government we have been  since 2010 to close that attainment gap  and that's what all our reforms have  been about the reforms to reading the  reforms to how you teach math the  changes to the curriculum in secondary  school the changes to the gcses and a  levels have all been about making sure  that every young person regardless of  their background gets the best possible  education okay minister university  students do you agree with your boss who  was on the show earlier on in the week  that they should receive a refund if  online learning continues  well what we said is that there's there  are no covet reasons now for  for students not to receive face-to-face  uh education these are matters  ultimately for for universities but  there's certainly no covered reason why  students cannot receive face-to-face  education the office for students the  regulator does monitor very closely the  quality of education  that students are receiving in our  universities and if students have  complaints they should of course  complain to the university and through  the complaints process  okay my question was should they receive  a refund your boss thinks they should do  you agree with him  well there can be that that there are  students who have gone through that  complaints procedure who have then  received conversation from the  university but these are ultimately a  matter  there are ultimately matters for the  university and we have a very powerful  regulator that gavin referred to i  understand all that i'm asking your  opinion do you agree with your boss that  kids who cannot have uh face-to-face  learning should get a refund  as i've said there is a procedure by  which they can get compensation  and that they need to go through the  complaints proceeding if the quality of  education they are receiving isn't of  the standard that is required by the  regulator okay so if that's the case and  lots of them do that who's going to pay  for the shortfall  well that will be the university sector  if they're not providing the quality of  education that the regulator requires of  them then of course the university  themselves will have to fund any  compensation payments made to to those  students but i am confident that we do  have very high quality education in our  universities we have a world-leading  university sector in this country which  we can be proud they have responded  extremely well uh to increasing numbers  of students going to university we've  seen a five percent increase last year  another five percent increase in places  this year we have record numbers of  young people going to university and  record numbers of young people from  disadvantaged backgrounds going to  university so i think we can be proud of  our university sector and what they're  doing for young people and what they're  doing for our country okay to that end  do you agree that universities should be  paying compensation for kids to defer  for 12 months so like exeter university  is offering 10 grand for some kids to do  fair for a year  well every year there are a number of  students who do a small number of  students who defer and that's the same  this year at universities we've been  working very closely  with you so you're not answering any of  my questions this morning do you think  that universities should pay students to  defer for a year  yes they can do if they wish to but  these things are always optional  for students any student that achieves i  need it on that one i just want to ask  you about your boss now um kyocera has  called for him to be sacked why do you  think he should keep his job  because he's a very effective education  secretary i've worked with gavin  williamson for the last two years and he  has driven to make sure that no student  will suffer long-term damage to their  prospect as a consequence of the  pandemic he's driven to continue the  reform program in our education system  where we're seeing up until covered we  saw that attainment gap close in primary  school and in secondary school and he's  driven to do more to make sure we  continue  to drive up standards in our education  system so that background does not  matter in terms of the opportunities  available for young people okay talk to  me about what's going on in berlin and a  british man arrested for uh spying uh  tobias elwood mp suggesting we need to  review the status of private security  personnel  do you agree  well i don't feel i'm able to talk about  this issue this is now subject  protecting the government this morning  police investigation  you're here representing the government  this morning  yes i am and because this is a matter of  police investigation it would not be  appropriate for any minister to come on  your program and to discuss this  particular issue okay i wonder if you  think that you have a view on the fact  that we don't have a security minister  that post has been open for a month uh  we should be filling that shouldn't we  given stuff like this is happening  well again this is the matter for for  the prime minister and uh he makes these  appointments and i'm sure that these  things will happen in due course okay  i'm going to ask you about the gurkhas  then has anyone from the government  spoken with representatives from the  gurkhas that are on hunger strike just  on whitehall there  well we do take very seriously the issue  of the pensions for gurkhas the gurkhas  pension scheme we've increased it  between 10 and 25 since march 2019 added  another 25 million pounds in terms of  providing health  for goku's in uh nepal  and you know ministers are of course in  touch with uh and we'll be in touch with  those people that are that are concerned  about the pensions for goku's the  gurkhas are a very important part of the  british army and we've always taken very  seriously uh the welfare and the  pensions of goku who've served in our in  our armed forces just to clarify you  said that ministers had been in touch  with them yeah  well i i'm not entirely sure who's met  who but i do know that we are always  keen to talk to people who are concerned  about the welfare of of of our of our  gurkha regiment and the and the uh and  the pensions arrangements for those for  those soldiers as i said we have been  we have increased the gurkhas pension  scheme  in march 2019 to make sure that we are  providing pensions for those  for those gurkhas who've served so well  in our armed forces indeed so 250  million quid for a boat for ministers to  travel around the world that's what the  prime minister is proposing while these  veterans are starving themselves to  death on the streets of uh whitehall the  optics are not good are they whatever  the rights and wrongs  the government has to do something about  this  and that would be a matter for the mod  in terms of wanting to sell britain  abroad it is important we are a global  trading nation  uh  and we need to make sure that we're  doing everything to sell british  business abroad and that's the purpose  of of of of of this of the kind of  projects that you're talking about  making sure that we are getting our  businesses exporting around the world uh  as we having left the european union and  that is what global britain is all about  okay quick thought before i let you go  your boss couldn't remember his a level  grades when he was asked  earlier on in the week can you remember  yours  i can yes if you uh yes i can remember  them yes go on tell  tell  me  oh well i got four four grey days and i  was able to go on to the university that  i hoped to go to and uh and and gavin  also was able to go to the university  that he  wanted to go to and he was very pleased  by his results it's great to talk to you  thanks for taking the time

